  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.25 × loss. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. A step is −learning rate × (slope + noise). Swings grow along x when the learning rate is above 0.5.",
    "Noise is Gaussian and centred on the true slope. Its spread is 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, like sampling b of 64 points, and it is exactly 0 at b = 64. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at their true step length, measured in bowl units from the ball's centre (scale 1). They point the way the ball moves, against the noisy or the true slope.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is diverged if the loss goes past 10 or the ball leaves the drawn square; the ball is then held at the bowl's edge height while the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses the next noise values and keeps the finished path as the ghost. The ball takes 5 steps a second at speed 1."
  ]
}